<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dankwarderode Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1160</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Burg Dankwarderode</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dankwarderode Castle is a castle in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> from about 1160 as his residence. In front of it stands the bronze Brunswick Lion, set up around 1166 as the symbol of his rule, beside the cathedral where he is buried. Rebuilt in Romanesque style in the late 19th century, it now houses the medieval collections of the Herzog <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ulrich-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anton Ulrich</a> Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dankwarderode-castle.jpg" alt="Dankwarderode Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Schäfer, Lich · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BurgDankwarderode2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
